Pipe P fills $1/10$ of the cistern per minute, and Pipe Q fills $1/15$. In the first 4 minutes, both work together. Their combined rate is $1/10 + 1/15 = 3/30 + 2/30 = 5/30 = 1/6$. In 4 minutes, they fill $4 \times (1/6) = 2/3$ of the cistern.

The remaining amount to fill is $1 - 2/3 = 1/3$. After Pipe P is turned off, only Pipe Q works at a rate of $1/15$ per minute. Let $t$ be the additional time needed. We solve $t \times (1/15) = 1/3$, which gives $t = (1/3) \times 15 = 5$. It will take 5 more minutes.

